1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,777
This is MATH 239, video 8. The topic is the generating series of a set with a weight function. This is Section 2.2.1 of the course notes.

2
00:00:09,227 --> 00:00:25,435
After this video, you can say when a function is a weight function. You can write the generating series of a set and read its coefficients. You can prove Proposition 2.7. And you can solve the practice, tutorial and assignment questions of this topic, and two questions of the practice midterm.

3
00:00:25,885 --> 00:00:40,002
Here is a counting problem with many answers. Count the subsets of 1, 2, 3 of each size. Each row of cells is one subset. A blue cell is an element of the subset. The subsets stand in the column of their size.

4
00:00:40,452 --> 00:00:50,075
The column heights are the counts. One subset of size 0, three of size 1, three of size 2, and one of size 3.

5
00:00:50,525 --> 00:01:07,848
Now write one term x to the size for each subset, and add the terms. The result is 1 plus 3 x plus 3 x squared plus x cubed. The coefficient of x to the n is the number of subsets of size n. So one power series holds all the counts.

6
00:01:08,298 --> 00:01:22,044
Section 2.1 did this for the subsets of 1 to n. The sum is 1 plus x, to the n. That is the Binomial Theorem. This video makes the idea general: any set, and any rule for the size of an object.

7
00:01:22,494 --> 00:01:40,234
Here are the coefficients of 1 plus x, cubed, as a row of cells. Cell n holds the coefficient of x to the n. The cells are 1, 3, 3, 1, and then zeros. These are the counts of the subsets of 1, 2, 3 by size.

8
00:01:40,684 --> 00:01:56,195
The second row is 1 over 1 minus x, cubed. Its coefficients count the multisets with 3 types of elements, by size. The Binomial Series gives the coefficient: n plus t minus 1, choose t minus 1.

9
00:01:56,645 --> 00:02:14,292
Look at x squared. A multiset with 3 types is a vector m 1, m 2, m 3 of natural numbers. Its size is the sum. There are 6 vectors with sum 2, and 4 choose 2 is 6. So in both rows, a coefficient counts objects of one size.

10
00:02:14,742 --> 00:02:24,723
In general, we want a sequence of numbers g 0, g 1, g 2, and so on. For example, g n can be the number of objects of size n.

11
00:02:25,173 --> 00:02:32,186
We put g n in front of x to the n, and add the terms. The result G of x is the generating series of the sequence g.

12
00:02:32,636 --> 00:02:44,571
Here x is an indeterminate. It has no value. The power of x only marks the position of each coefficient. So x keeps the numbers g n apart. Chapter 4 shows how to get the numbers back out of the series.

13
00:02:45,021 --> 00:03:09,495
Now the definitions. Take a set S of objects. A function w from S to the natural numbers gives each object a weight. w is a weight function if, for every n, only finitely many objects have weight n. S n is the set of objects of weight n. The notes write script A and omega. The course uses S and w.

14
00:03:09,945 --> 00:03:21,872
Example. Let S be the set of all binary strings, and let the weight be the length. The empty string epsilon has length 0. The strings 0 and 1 have length 1.

15
00:03:22,322 --> 00:03:24,319
There are 4 strings of length 2.

16
00:03:24,769 --> 00:03:26,673
And there are 8 strings of length 3.

17
00:03:27,123 --> 00:03:37,758
The counts are 1, 2, 4, 8. In general, S n has 2 to the n strings. Each S n is finite, so the length is a weight function on the binary strings.

18
00:03:38,208 --> 00:03:52,929
Every object has exactly one weight. So S is the union of S 0, S 1, S 2, and so on, and these sets do not overlap. A countable union of finite sets is finite or countably infinite.

19
00:03:53,379 --> 00:04:03,457
The finite condition can fail. Practice problem P2-4 part b: all binary strings, and the weight v is twice the number of 0s. Pause the video and try it.

20
00:04:03,907 --> 00:04:10,501
A string with no 0s has weight 0. The empty string, 1, and 1 1 all have weight 0.

21
00:04:10,951 --> 00:04:24,883
So do 1 1 1, 1 1 1 1, and every string of only 1s. There is one for every length. So the set of weight 0 is infinite, and v is not a weight function. The weight 2 set is also infinite.

22
00:04:25,333 --> 00:04:42,910
Tutorial 2, question 1 part b has the same problem. Take tuples with entries 1, 2, 3, of any length. The weight counts the places where 1 is followed by 1. Every tuple of only 2s has weight 0. So again infinitely many tuples have weight 0.

23
00:04:43,360 --> 00:04:57,059
Why must each S n be finite? The coefficient of x to the n will be the number of objects of weight n. That must be a natural number. Infinitely many terms x to the 0 do not give a coefficient.

24
00:04:57,509 --> 00:05:12,695
Definition 2.6. The generating series of S with respect to w is phi S w of x. It is the sum, over all sigma in S, of x to the w of sigma. Here is the set of the 8 subsets of 1, 2, 3.

25
00:05:13,145 --> 00:05:22,991
Take the weight w of sigma equal to the size of sigma. The empty set has weight 0. The three single elements have weight 1, and so on.

26
00:05:23,441 --> 00:05:33,611
Each object gives one term: x to its weight. So the empty set gives x to the 0, and the set 1, 2 gives x squared.

27
00:05:34,061 --> 00:05:38,519
Add the 8 terms. This sum is the definition, written out.

28
00:05:38,969 --> 00:05:57,127
Collect the terms with equal powers. Phi S of x equals 1 plus 3 x plus 3 x squared plus x cubed. Check: put x equal to 1. Each term becomes 1, so the result is the number of objects, 8. And 1 plus 3 plus 3 plus 1 is 8.

29
00:05:57,577 --> 00:06:05,704
Now keep the same 8 subsets, but change the weight. The weight of sigma is now the sum of its elements. The empty set has sum 0.

30
00:06:06,154 --> 00:06:15,721
Put each subset in the column of its sum. The set 3 and the set 1, 2 both have sum 3. The set 1, 2, 3 has sum 6.

31
00:06:16,171 --> 00:06:25,737
Count each column. The series is 1 plus x plus x squared plus 2 x cubed plus x to the 4 plus x to the 5 plus x to the 6.

32
00:06:26,187 --> 00:06:38,401
The set is the same, but the series is different. At x equal to 1, both series give 8. So the generating series depends on the set and on the weight. In a solution, always state both.

33
00:06:38,851 --> 00:06:53,619
Proposition 2.7 says why this works. If phi A of x has coefficients a n, then a n is the number of objects of weight n. The proof starts with the definition. The chips are the terms of the example on the last slide, in any order.

34
00:06:54,069 --> 00:07:02,614
Group the objects by weight. Every object has exactly one weight n, so each term goes into exactly one group. The groups are the columns.

35
00:07:03,064 --> 00:07:13,141
In group n, every term is x to the n. So x to the n comes out of the inner sum. What is left is a sum of 1s, one for each object of weight n.

36
00:07:13,591 --> 00:07:28,313
A sum of 1s over A n is the size of A n. So the coefficient of x to the n is the number of objects of weight n. Each A n is finite, so each coefficient is a natural number. This is where the weight function condition is used.

37
00:07:28,763 --> 00:07:37,215
So a generating series is a list of counts. Count the objects of weight n, or read the coefficient of x to the n. The two give the same number.

38
00:07:37,665 --> 00:07:53,269
We need a short name for one coefficient. Definition 2.8: the coefficient of x to the k in G of x is written as x to the k, in square brackets, then G of x. For 1 over 1 minus x, every coefficient is 1.

39
00:07:53,719 --> 00:08:06,490
Example 2.9 uses the Binomial Series with t equal to 1 plus b. The coefficient of x to the n is n plus b, choose b. So the coefficient of x to the a is a plus b, choose b.

40
00:08:06,940 --> 00:08:22,404
For example, take 1 over 1 minus x, to the 4. Then 1 plus b is 4, so b is 3. For a equal to 2, the coefficient is 5 choose 3, which is 10. The row shows 10 in the x squared cell.

41
00:08:22,854 --> 00:08:32,043
With this notation, Proposition 2.7 is one line. The coefficient of x to the n in phi S of x is the number of objects of weight n.

42
00:08:32,493 --> 00:08:48,840
Here is the method for a small set. List the objects. Find the weight of each one. Write one term x to the weight for each object. Collect the equal powers. Then check: at x equal to 1, the sum must be the number of objects.

43
00:08:49,290 --> 00:09:01,875
For a large or infinite set, you cannot list every object. Then find the count of objects of weight n for a general n, and use it as the coefficient of x to the n. The next questions use both ways.

44
00:09:02,325 --> 00:09:17,325
Practice problem P1-8. S is the set of tuples of length 5 with exactly two 1s. The weight is the number of 0s between the two 1s. Find the generating series. Pause the video and try it. There are 5 choose 2, which is 10, tuples.

45
00:09:17,775 --> 00:09:27,087
Mark the 0s between the 1s in orange. Their number is the weight. For example, 1 0 0 0 1 has weight 3.

46
00:09:27,537 --> 00:09:31,810
Sort the tuples by weight. Weight 0 means the two 1s are next to each other.

47
00:09:32,260 --> 00:09:39,923
Count each column. There are 4 tuples of weight 0, 3 of weight 1, 2 of weight 2, and 1 of weight 3.

48
00:09:40,373 --> 00:09:48,964
So phi S of x equals 4 plus 3 x plus 2 x squared plus x cubed. Check: 4 plus 3 plus 2 plus 1 is 10.

49
00:09:49,414 --> 00:10:05,854
For n tuples, weight k means the second 1 is k plus 1 places after the first 1. The first 1 can start in n minus k minus 1 places. So the coefficient of x to the k is n minus 1 minus k, for k from 0 to n minus 2.

50
00:10:06,304 --> 00:10:32,821
Tutorial 2, question 1 part a. S 4 is the set of tuples of length 4 with entries 1, 2 or 3. The weight is the number of places where a 1 is followed by a 1. Pause the video and try it. The weight depends only on where the 1s are. So first choose the positions of the 1s. Every other entry is 2 or 3. A pattern with k ones gives 2 to the 4 minus k tuples.

51
00:10:33,271 --> 00:10:47,946
Weight 0. The 1s are never next to each other. No 1 gives 16 tuples. One 1 has 4 positions, with 8 tuples each. Two 1s apart have 3 patterns, with 4 tuples each.

52
00:10:48,396 --> 00:11:02,142
Weight 1. Two 1s next to each other: 3 patterns, with 4 tuples each. Or three 1s with one gap: 1 1 star 1, and 1 star 1 1. Each gives 2 tuples.

53
00:11:02,592 --> 00:11:10,812
Weight 2: 1 1 1 star and star 1 1 1, with 2 tuples each. Weight 3: only 1 1 1 1.

54
00:11:11,262 --> 00:11:24,358
Add the tuples in each column. Weight 0: 16 plus 32 plus 12 is 60. Weight 1: 12 plus 4 is 16. Weight 2: 4. Weight 3: 1.

55
00:11:24,808 --> 00:11:36,596
So the generating series is 60 plus 16 x plus 4 x squared plus x cubed. Check: the sum is 81, and there are 3 to the 4, which is 81, tuples.

56
00:11:37,046 --> 00:11:57,201
Practice problem P2-4 part a. Now the strings have a fixed length n, and v is twice the number of 0s. Pause the video and try it. B n has 2 to the n elements. A finite set always has finitely many objects of each weight. So v is a weight function.

57
00:11:57,651 --> 00:12:02,713
Look at n equal to 3. The 0s are orange. The weight is twice the number of orange cells.

58
00:12:03,163 --> 00:12:08,597
The counts are 1, 0, 3, 0, 3, 0, 1. Odd weights do not occur.

59
00:12:09,047 --> 00:12:26,462
In general, choose the k positions of the 0s in n choose k ways. Each such string has weight 2 k. So the series is the sum of n choose k, x to the 2 k. By the Binomial Theorem with x squared in place of x, this is 1 plus x squared, to the n.

60
00:12:26,912 --> 00:12:43,537
Assignment 1, problem 4 part a. S is the set of the 16 subsets of 0, 1, 2, 3. The weight w is the sum of the elements. The weight w star is the product. The empty set has weight 0 for both. Pause the video and try it.

61
00:12:43,987 --> 00:12:55,644
First w. Put each subset in the column of its sum. The column of 3 holds the sets 3, 1 2, and the same two sets with 0 added.

62
00:12:56,094 --> 00:13:07,936
Count the columns. The series is 2 plus 2 x plus 2 x squared plus 4 x cubed plus 2 x to the 4 plus 2 x to the 5 plus 2 x to the 6.

63
00:13:08,386 --> 00:13:18,974
Every count is even. The element 0 does not change the sum. So each subset without 0 has a partner with 0, in the same column.

64
00:13:19,424 --> 00:13:33,449
Now the product weight w star. Every subset that contains 0 has product 0. There are 8 such subsets. The empty set also has weight 0. So 9 subsets have weight 0. Pause the video and try the rest.

65
00:13:33,899 --> 00:13:45,648
Sort the subsets by product. The set 1 has product 1. The sets 2 and 1 2 have product 2. The sets 2 3 and 1 2 3 have product 6.

66
00:13:46,098 --> 00:13:59,321
So the series for w star is 9 plus x plus 2 x squared plus 2 x cubed plus 2 x to the 6. Check: 9 plus 1 plus 2 plus 2 plus 2 is 16.

67
00:13:59,771 --> 00:14:11,845
Part b. Now T is the set of all finite subsets of the natural numbers. Are the two series formal power series? That is the same as: is each weight a weight function? Pause the video and try it.

68
00:14:12,295 --> 00:14:29,199
For the sum w. A set with sum n cannot contain an element larger than n. So it is a subset of 0 to n. There are at most 2 to the n plus 1 such sets. So each T n is finite, and the series is a formal power series.

69
00:14:29,649 --> 00:14:40,052
For the product w star. Every set that contains 0 has product 0. The sets 0 1, 0 2, 0 3, and so on, all have weight 0.

70
00:14:40,502 --> 00:14:48,675
So infinitely many sets have weight 0. The constant term is not a number, and the series for w star is not a formal power series.

71
00:14:49,125 --> 00:15:05,193
Practice midterm, question 4 part a. S 3 is the set of the 7 nonempty subsets of 1, 2, 3. The weight w is the smallest element. The weight v is the largest element. Pause the video and try it.

72
00:15:05,643 --> 00:15:17,300
Sort by the smallest element. Four sets contain 1, so they have minimum 1. The sets 2 and 2 3 have minimum 2. Only the set 3 has minimum 3.

73
00:15:17,750 --> 00:15:27,363
Sort by the largest element. Only the set 1 has maximum 1. The sets 2 and 1 2 have maximum 2. Four sets have maximum 3.

74
00:15:27,813 --> 00:15:48,293
So phi w is 4 x plus 2 x squared plus x cubed, and phi v is x plus 2 x squared plus 4 x cubed. In general, minimum k leaves the n minus k larger elements free, so there are 2 to the n minus k sets. Maximum k leaves the k minus 1 smaller elements free.

75
00:15:48,743 --> 00:16:00,724
Part b. T is now the set of finite subsets of the natural numbers. The empty set has no minimum or maximum, so we use the nonempty subsets. Pause the video and try it.

76
00:16:01,174 --> 00:16:20,493
For the minimum. The sets 2, 2 3, 2 4, 2 3 4, 2 5, and so on, all have minimum 2. There is no upper limit on the other elements. So infinitely many sets have weight 2, and the series for w is not a formal power series.

77
00:16:20,943 --> 00:16:33,993
For the maximum. A set with maximum n is n, together with any subset Y of 0 to n minus 1. There are n such numbers, so there are 2 to the n choices for Y. Each T n is finite.

78
00:16:34,443 --> 00:16:44,195
So v is a weight function, and the series for v is the sum of 2 to the n, x to the n. By the geometric series, this is 1 over 1 minus 2 x.

79
00:16:44,645 --> 00:16:56,116
Practice midterm, question 5. S is the set of the numbers 0 to 50. The weight of a is 4 a plus 3. Find the generating series as a simplified rational expression. Pause the video and try it.

80
00:16:56,566 --> 00:17:07,201
Each a gives one term, x to the 4 a plus 3. So 0 gives x cubed, 1 gives x to the 7, and 50 gives x to the 203.

81
00:17:07,651 --> 00:17:20,747
As a row of coefficients, there is a 1 at positions 3, 7, 11, 15, and so on. All other coefficients are 0. Each weight occurs at most once, so w is a weight function.

82
00:17:21,197 --> 00:17:44,835
Take out x cubed. What is left is a finite geometric sum in x to the 4, with 51 terms. The sum of r to the a, for a from 0 to 50, is 1 minus r to the 51, over 1 minus r. With r equal to x to the 4, the result is x cubed times 1 minus x to the 204, over 1 minus x to the 4.

83
00:17:45,285 --> 00:17:58,520
Practice problem P2-3. We know the generating series, and we want the number of elements of weight 15. Pause the video and try it. By Proposition 2.7, the answer is the coefficient of x to the 15.

84
00:17:58,970 --> 00:18:12,986
x over 1 minus 2 x, to the 7, is x to the 7 times 1 minus 2 x to the minus 7. The factor x to the 7 moves every coefficient 7 places. So we need the coefficient of x to the 8 in the rest.

85
00:18:13,436 --> 00:18:26,857
Call c k the coefficient of x to the k in 1 minus 2 x, to the minus 7. The Binomial Series with 2 x in place of x gives c k equals k plus 6 choose 6, times 2 to the k.

86
00:18:27,307 --> 00:18:34,320
Multiply by 1 plus x. The 1 gives the row of c k. The x gives the same row, moved one place right.

87
00:18:34,770 --> 00:18:48,980
The column of x to the 8 holds c 8 and c 7. So the answer is 14 choose 6 times 2 to the 8, plus 13 choose 6 times 2 to the 7. This is the posted answer. You do not need to evaluate it.

88
00:18:49,430 --> 00:19:05,397
On a test, you must justify each answer. A full answer has four parts. Define the set. Define the weight, and check that it is a weight function. Find the generating series, and name each result that you use. Then read the coefficient.

89
00:19:05,847 --> 00:19:15,878
Here is a full answer to P1-8. It defines S and w. It says why w is a weight function. It gives the counts of each weight, and then the series.

90
00:19:16,328 --> 00:19:26,916
When you count with a series, say why the coefficient is the answer: by Proposition 2.7. The practice midterm asks you to name each result that you use.

91
00:19:27,366 --> 00:19:39,069
To recap. A weight function gives each object a natural number, with only finitely many objects of each weight. If one weight has infinitely many objects, there is no power series.

92
00:19:39,519 --> 00:19:50,061
The generating series has one term x to the weight for each object. By Proposition 2.7, the coefficient of x to the n is the number of objects of weight n.

93
00:19:50,511 --> 00:20:04,721
For a small set, list the objects and collect the terms. For a large set, count the objects of weight n in general. The next video gives the Sum, Product and String Lemmas. They build the series of a large set from the series of smaller sets.
